Carry a resolution record: a definition's uses list names what its bodies say short

Dev accepted, on 2026-10-21, "the resolution record: a definition carries a `uses` list of the
identities its bodies name short, written by compact and validated against the ontology, so an
unscoped reader resolves the file from itself; the rest of the sweep follows it." The gap it
closes showed itself while building: the unscoped GeneratorCLI, generating TypeDefinition, loaded
the swept TypeName and misjudged its payload, since nothing in the file said which Dracon.

TypeDefinition gains `uses [TypeName]`, default empty and elided, beside typeName; its trio is
regenerated, and TypeLoader's rooting reconstruction and the generator's target-type rebuild carry
the slot (the first did not, and the record never reached the engine until it did — the §6 rule
about rebuilds, once more). On the surface `uses [ … ]` is a section of its own between the type
section and the domain line, each entry in the anchor spelling — `draco format dracon Dracon` —
written by the compact form and never by hand; `uses` is reserved. NameResolution.compactJson
writes it: every identity of the ontology that a body names by its short form, other than the
definition's own package, sorted; a definition spelling everything in full carries none. Every
scoped read validates it — an entry the ontology does not define is an error — and a resolved
definition carries no record, every name being in full, so compact recomputes it.
NameResolution.recorded(td) is the scope a definition carries in itself: one placeholder domain
per package of the record, no bindings, lenient, so a leaf the record does not name passes as
written. The engine's unscoped generation spells a recorded definition through that scope exactly
as a scoped generation would and shortens by import; its loader does the same for a recorded
definition it loads with no scope in effect. So, for the seventeen swept definitions, unscoped
and scoped generation are identical and equal the pinned Scala, and their referrers generate
unscoped again; the ontology is needed only to author and validate the record. A definition
spelled in full and unswept still generates as its author spelled it, imports and all only under
a scope — the rest of the sweep removes that asymmetry.

The seventeen swept files are re-swept and carry their records (TypeName's: Absent, Presence,
Present, Dracon). ResolutionRecordTest (4) is the 60th suite: the record is written, sorted,
idempotent and round-trips the surface; unscoped equals scoped for every swept definition and
their pinned files; the recorded scope passes unknown names; a stale entry fails every scoped read.
No corpus type changed, so no headline moves.

Probe on the compiled engine: 29 suites, 766 passed, zero failed or aborted, every headline
unchanged, pending mods actors 0; the whole main and mods tree compiles against the regenerated
classes; GeneratorCLI's unscoped generation of TypeName, TypeDefinition, Problem and CirceCodecs
equals the pinned Scala.

Gate: Dev's full sbt run on 2026-10-21, pasted, green on its first run: 849 tests / 60 suites, zero
failed, canceled, ignored or pending tests, zero aborted suites; every report-only headline
unchanged; pending mods actors 0 in the file-only log. Committed and pushed by Dev.

